<exercise>
<!-- source latex = 'floor function question.tex' -->
<!-- converted on 24/06/2021 at 14:47 -->
<statement>
<p>
The <em>floor</em> function, denoted by <m>\lfloor x \rfloor </m>, is defined to be the function that takes a real number <m>x</m> and returns the greatest integer less than or equal to <m>x</m>. This is also sometimes called the <em>greatest integer function</em>. For example,
<me>
\lfloor 3.5 \rfloor=3,
\qquad
\lfloor -2.5 \rfloor=-3,
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\lfloor 7 \rfloor=7.
</me>
Using this definition, prove that
<me>
\lfloor x \rfloor=x
\implies
x\in\mathbb{Z}</me>,
and that
<me>
x\in\mathbb{Z}
\implies
  \lfloor x \rfloor=x.
</me>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>

    <proof><p>
      Start by letting <m>x \in \mathbb{R}</m> and write <m>n = \lfloor x \rfloor</m>. By definition of the floor function <m>n</m> is an integer.
    </p>
      <p>
        Now we prove each implication in turn. 
<ul>
<li> <p>Assume that <m>n = x</m>. By definition of the floor function <m>n</m> is an integer, so <m>x</m> is an integer as required. </p></li>
<li>
            <p>Now assume that <m>x \in \mathbb{Z}</m>. We prove the result by showing that <m>n \leq x</m> and <m>n \geq x</m>.
            <ul>
              <li> Then, since the floor function returns the greatest integer less than or equal to <m>x</m>, we know that <m>n \leq x</m>. </li>
              <li> By definition of the floor function, we know that for any integer <m>k</m> satsifying <m>k \leq x</m>, we must have that <m>k \leq n</m>,
              since <m>n</m> is the greatest such integer. Since <m>x</m> is an integer and <m>x \leq x</m>, we know that <m>x \leq n</m> </li>
            </ul>
           Because <m>x \leq n</m> and <m>x \geq n</m>, we know <m>x = n = \lfloor x \rfloor</m>.
  </p></li>
</ul>
</p></proof>
<p>
  What we are really doing here is proving a <q>biconditional statement</q>. We will see much more on this in the next couple of chapters.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>
